# The Highwayman — The Legend of the Five Rings 2019 Gen Con Adventure

**Version:** 0.4
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings Roleplaying Game — The Highwayman: The 2019 L5R Gen Con Adventure
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games (2020)

> GM-facing narrative for the adventure. Boxed **read-aloud** text is reproduced verbatim;
> intervening GM procedure (check TNs, terrain, branch bookkeeping) is summarized faithfully — the
> full procedural detail lives in the source PDF. The Dramatis Personae statblocks are in
> `l5r5e-0.4-highwayman-cast.ttrpg`, the six pregens in `l5r5e-0.4-highwayman-pregens.actor`, and the
> act structure in `l5r5e-0.4-highwayman.arc`. This adventure lets players take the roles of gaijin,
> rōnin, and peasant characters — the outsiders of Rokugan.

---

## Adventure Background

Deep in the mysterious Shinomen Forest, the Shadowlands Taint has taken root. Legends of a brutal Highwayman have spread amongst local peasants and farmers, passing in drunken whispers in the smoky light of the evening. Bloody sigils carved into trees and marked on doors are whispered to plague those brave enough to pass through the Shinomen.

In *The Highwayman*, the PCs investigate the disappearance of a missing shipment of sake on behalf of the merchant Ito Moronoka. What starts out as a straightforward search for some missing goods spirals into a deadly otherworldly fight for their lives against the dreaded Highwayman. The adventure begins when the PCs arrive at a small town outside of the Shinomen in search of the missing courier Hidetaka Nori and, more importantly, the exorbitantly expensive sake he was supposed to deliver to Moronoka.

The Shinomen Forest is a mysterious, sprawling tangle of ancient trees that dwarfs most Great Clan castles and holdings. It has endured long before the establishment of the Chrysanthemum Throne and will continue long after Rokugan has fallen. Under cover of darkness, the wicked creatures who seek destruction have crept into the heart of the Shinomen where they wait patiently. One such creature — the rakshasa who masquerades as the Highwayman — has invited two oni to be her honored guests at a final banquet before they sow discord in the rest of Rokugan. Following a lead to Nori's whereabouts, the PCs find a lavish estate deep in the Shinomen, and are invited by Lady Yukiko (who is actually the fabled Highwayman) to sit down to a decadent banquet with her two guests, who are oni in disguise. Lady Yukiko herself is a shapeshifting, reality-bending rakshasa with a taste for human flesh. The PCs must exercise wisdom and careful judgment to escape intact, and to prevent the growing power of Lady Yukiko spreading across Rokugan.

---

## Act 1: The Missing Courier

The PCs arrive at **Kawacho**, a small town controlled by the Soshi through a vassal family, built near very fertile land and known for the excellent sake produced at its small riverside distillery. They come seeking the missing courier **Hidetaka Nori** and the six crates of expensive sake he was to deliver to the merchant **Ito Moronoka**.

At the Hidetaka home, Nori's mother **Sazuko** and sister **Sasami** worry over him: Sazuko injured her foot gathering water, and Nori took half a day to gather herbs for a poultice for her, then left half a day late and took a shortcut through the Shinomen to make up the time. He was last seen leaving the village by his brother, **Sasuke**, at the sake house, five days ago.

### The Local Sake House

The local sake house is filled with laughter and delicious sake. Its burly, thick-chested bartender introduces himself:

> The thick-armed bartender smiles jovially at you. "I am Hidetaka Sasuke, owner of this sake house," he thumbs his chest proudly. "Let me know how I can help make your time in Kawacho something you will remember—or not." He grins as he points at the row of sake behind him.

Sasuke is suspicious of strangers asking about his family (a TN 3 Courtesy check, reduced if the PCs mention Sasami sent them). He recalls that Nori came in "like a whirlwind about five days ago, stressing out about his boss being angry. Something about a wedding," and that against advice he took the shorter path through the Shinomen, "foolish man." Several townspeople mumble about bad luck and the Highwayman.

### The Highwayman (local legend)

Local legends of a brutal Highwayman live on in the imaginations of the locals, who throw out successively more ridiculous claims at the sake house:

- "If one travels after dark, the Highwayman shall surely devour them. Do not travel at night through the Shinomen."
- "Everywhere he goes, he leaves his mark. It looks like a crescent moon."
- "I heard the Highwayman is a wolf, perhaps a deer, possessed by the spirit of a lovelorn samurai."
- "It is said he eats the hearts of his victims to remain strong forever."
- "Folks believe the Highwayman is some mythical monster, but the truth is it is just a bunch of bandits hiding behind a legend."

The good sake Nori carried has a white and blue label sealed with the symbol of Kawacho, a frog; a bottle of the good stuff is two bu, the excellent stuff Moronoka orders costs a koku.

### Optional: Today I am Sayaka

As the PCs are about to leave, a cloaked, muscular woman motions them over. Her clothing suggests she is a merchant, but she looks out of place in Scorpion lands — skin the color of dark honey, hair in thick braids, hands heavily callused, bearing no Clan colors. She calls herself **Sayaka** (though she changes her name often; she was originally named Bataar Badma, from the Burning Sands, and lived in the shadow of Khanbulak). Treated respectfully, she shares what she knows: she has explored the outskirts of the Shinomen and mentions small, shy spirits (kodama) and an area where a "moaning tree" lives; a path of red markings on many trees is upsetting the spirits; shipments of food and goods have gone missing, blamed on the Highwayman. She may agree to act as the group's guide.

---

## Act 2: Exploring the Shinomen

Following Nori's trail into the Shinomen:

> As you enter the sea of ancient trees, following the worn path, the foliage overhead grows denser and denser, letting in less and less light. Keeping to the path is difficult, and something feels off about how quiet the forest is. The trees seem to watch your every movement with palpable anticipation.

The Shinomen is a strange, ancient place that is not inherently hostile to outsiders but does not accommodate them either. The GM plays an unreliable narrator — describing a feature one way, then differently the next, portraying greater and greater inconsistencies to unsettle the PCs. Along the way the PCs may encounter:

- **The Mark of the Highwayman:** the forest is marked with numerous red crescent-moon symbols.
- **Okuri-inu:** supernatural wolf-dogs (a TN 2 Theology check reveals them) that flit in the shadows and wait for travelers to lose their footing before pouncing; a sure-footed party is safe.
- **Trees / Vegetation & Signs of Life:** welcoming boughs that smother and tangle; a magnificent buck that, if left alone, removes strife from the beautiful moment, but if approached contorts into a horrid visage and vanishes.
- **Kodama (Forest Spirits):** wispy, soft-hearted forest kami who dwell in the ancient trees, emerging under darkness to play in the treetops. The PCs may find them mourning a tree marked with the Highwayman's sign, or offering help when the party is lost.

Following Nori's trail eventually leads to the courier's overturned, sky-blue wagon deep in the forest, where the spiritual disturbances are strong.

---

## Act 3: The Estate

Following the signs, the PCs arrive at a large estate in a clearing. This is all an illusion created by the rakshasa; in reality, the estate is built on a fetid marsh that smells like bilious tar.

> As the trees break, you are greeted by a sprawling complex of ornately carved buildings of a grand estate. Overhead, the lidless eye of the moon casts the place in a ghostly pallor. A short walkway lit with braziers leads to the front entrance of the main hall. There are no gates or walls here. Tranquil music streams out of an open door on the cool night breeze. The scent on the air, however, is that of a vile marsh.

Approaching the entrance, a beautiful young woman in a lavish pink kimono emerges with a train of servants:

> "It appears we have more guests!" She clasps her hands together as a broad smile plays across her painted face. "Come in, come in! The night is cold and the forest is filled with ravenous beasts. But you are welcome in the estate of Lady Kuriyama Yukiko." She bows, "We were about to enjoy a grand feast!"

### The Illusion

Yukiko's mute servants appear to be normal, living human servants in ornate robes; a TN 3 Medicine or Theology check may reveal they are actually undead — the smell of rotting flesh, spiritual ague, decayed skin and bones. Any clothing or gifts the PCs accept will later turn into withered rags and rotted wood when the estate vanishes. Small details undermine the PCs' perception: constant gnats, the smell of swamp water, intense cold and warmth in places, an occasional shimmer revealing the reality around them.

If the party does not tell Yukiko they are looking for Nori, she raises it herself: she claims to have recently saved a young man from Kawacho, found bloody and beaten on the path, who "should be well enough to travel home soon."

### The Feast

The banquet is an intrigue: whether the PCs eat, whether they see through the illusion, and whether they trust Yukiko's offer to take them to Nori. Her honored guests are the oni **Lady Atsuko** (who sips tea with utter disdain) and **Lord Kenta** (who munches thoughtlessly on cartilage). If the PCs refuse her hospitality, Yukiko's restraint wanes:

> Lady Yukiko stands abruptly, brushing out the pleats in her kimono in one harsh movement. She surveys those of you who have not touched your food and scowls. "This is no good. To receive you in my own home, to offer you the fruits of my labors: for you to reject my hospitality like this?" Her eyes narrow. "I will not suffer this dishonor."

If all the PCs indulge and fail to convince her to take them to Nori, she saves "the best part of the feast" — reaching into a roast pig's belly for its boiled heart and offering it to the most reluctant guest (a PC who accepts receives 4 strife). Whether trusted (she leads them to a garden that melts into a fetid marsh) or refused (the manor shimmers away), the illusion drops and Yukiko is revealed:

> The beautiful garden melts before your eyes into a fetid marsh. You turn to Yukiko and are greeted by a creature that towers a full head taller than you. Moonlight glints on a sleek, scaled torso, matted with dark blood, supported by black sinewy legs. It has lanky, spindly arms and yellowed claws; rather than a face, her head is dominated with a large, fang-ridden mouth. Yellow, slitted eyes stare down at you, "Prepare to meet Nori, children."

### Exploring the Estate & the Skirmish

The PCs may explore rather than (or before) fighting. Almost all items and decorations are illusions that lose their form if handled. The main estate — a large outcropping in a festering swamp once the illusion falls — holds the **Banquet Hall** (bordered in the bones of dozens of creatures; Defiled and Entangling terrain), the **Atrium** (where **Lord Kenta**, the wolf-headed oni, waits and challenges a PC to a wrestling test of strength), the **Kitchens** (a grisly larder of real human remains and five crates of the missing sake; Defiled and Obscured), and **Atsuko's Shrine** between two guesthouses (where **Lady Atsuko** meditates, and an intrigue may convince her to leave the Shinomen, revealing her jealousy of Yukiko and her vulnerability to fire). The confrontation resolves as skirmishes against Yukiko, Atsuko, and Kenta as the PCs' choices dictate.

---

## Act 4: Conclusion

The survivors return. In **Kawacho**, tales of the macabre banquet make the villagers loath to investigate the forest — though a TN 4 Command check may rouse them to strike out against the Highwayman's dwelling.

At **Ito Moronoka's** shop, the merchant eyes what the PCs bring back. If they return with four or five crates of the sake — "enough for the wedding" — and explain the loss well (a Courtesy check), Moronoka believes them, pays their full **6 koku** each, and they gain the **Ally [Ito Moronoka]** distinction. Fewer crates, or a failed explanation, means less pay (down to 2–4 koku) and no distinction; pressing Moronoka for more money risks forfeiting honor for breaching Courtesy and summoning his workers (such as the burly **Asuka**).

**Rewards:** 3 XP for completing the adventure and 1 XP per intrigue, skirmish, or duel completed; 4 Glory for defeating each of the three major enemies — Lady Yukiko, Lady Atsuko, and Lord Kenta (12 Glory possible); and 4 Honor for wrestling Lord Kenta alone.

---

## Player Handouts

The adventure ships six pregenerated non-samurai characters — gaijin, rōnin, peasant, and monk — each with a private handout carrying their backstory, and each with pre-written relationships to the rest of the party (full statistics and relationships in `l5r5e-0.4-highwayman-pregens.actor`).

### Ahuja Mishti — Gaijin (Ivory Kingdoms Sage)

From the Ivory Kingdoms, far to the southwest of Rokugan, Mishti is part of a special order of warriors called the **Yodha**, dedicated to keeping the threats of the Ghostlands' forests (the southern region of the Shadowlands) out of their homeland. Trained from childhood to hunt wicked creatures, she knows the Yodha's greatest enemies are the **rakshasa** — shapeshifting demons that revel in tricking and eating humans. A little over a year ago, Mishti failed to recognize a rakshasa wearing the form of her grandmother and let it into their home; it slaughtered her entire family, stole their sacred blade, and cruelly let her live to suffer. She has tracked the rakshasa to Scorpion lands and become fascinated by Rokugani culture, torn between revenge and a secret longing to simply live free of supernatural horrors. (This ties her directly to the adventure's antagonist, the rakshasa Lady Yukiko.) *Roleplaying:* cautious around strangers, watchful for deception, deliberate of speech; once she truly befriends someone she treats them as her own blood.

### Akagi Daichi — Peasant (a hinin's secret)

Daichi grew up in a small village near the Crab Clan's Kaiu Wall, born into the **hinin** caste and made to feel inferior, tasked with spiritually staining work. He took to pottery and art — considered radical for his station; when the village head found his work she nearly cut off his hand. He ran away to become a traveling artist, hiding his origins, longing for lasting relationships yet fearing exposure. He has worked with this group for a little over two months transporting goods to a distant Crab city; the promise of 6 koku for one job — almost unfathomable wealth for someone of his birth — could finally let him open his own shop. *Roleplaying:* amicable and friendly, caring little for status differences, trying to see the best in others because he knows what it is to be treated unfairly.

### Hiyabayashi Kenshin — Commoner shugenja (former geisha)

Raised in a geisha house in Scorpion lands, Kenshin admired the artistry of the geisha of the **Red Lotus** and mastered it. Having saved enough to open their own geisha house in another city, they were overwhelmed by costs and took a substantial loan from **Nakamura Jun'ichi**; then their building burned down (possibly a competitor's meddling), and the debt kept climbing. Kenshin has an innate ability to call out to the kami, letting them take on dangerous but high-paying work to repay the debt while longing for fame, wealth, and powerful contacts. *Roleplaying:* speaks with flourish and flowery phrasing, loves luxury and attention, but wise enough to quiet down where the debtor's underlings might be watching.

### Maki Haruko — Rōnin (disgraced Lion)

Once **Matsu Megumi**, a samurai of the Matsu family of the Lion Clan — honorable, loyal, a candidate to become a general. But as she reached her gempuku she developed a respiratory illness, and her parents redirected her toward a courtier's life, never the battlefield. Crushed and longing for war, she stowed away with a troop of soldiers and took control of the squad to prove herself; her inexperience and illness overcame her and several soldiers died. Her parents discovered the reckless act and disowned her. Now she wanders Rokugan as the rōnin Haruko, trying to prove to her lost family and herself that she is worthy of redemption. *Roleplaying:* solid and unwavering, speaks only when it matters, puts herself between harm and her allies, never breaks a promise or returns with an incomplete job — she would rather die than fail again.

### Noburo — Monk (Mystic of the Mountain)

Noboru was raised as a monk from a young age in the **Mountains of Regret**, finding the rhythms of temple life comforting — not knowing the abbot's ideals verged on heretical. On the eve of their thirteenth year, bushi from a distant lord stormed the temple, burning it down and killing many within; Noboru escaped the blaze. Years later they hold no resentment toward the bushi who followed orders, but anger at the institution that allowed the killing. They have committed to exploring the Way, testing the Truth, and helping others toward enlightenment, fearing the horrors they witnessed will keep them from ever finding real peace. *Roleplaying:* offers advice even unrequested, loves to share wisdom, stories, and proverbs; humble, and fulfilled by helping others find their own Truth.

### Otgontogoch Turgen — Gaijin (Haunted Ujik)

Turgen left his homeland in the Burning Sands a little over a year ago with his brother **Sukhbatar**, after the two incurred the wrath of an angry shaman who cursed them to be haunted by twin spirits — Turgen's causing bad luck, his brother's causing temptation. Not wanting to plague their family, the pair set off for Rokugan; a month in, Turgen was betrayed by his brother and scarred, which he blames on his brother's tempting spirit. He now devotes himself to correcting spiritual imbalances, hoping to appease the spirit he calls **Bataar**, find Sukhbatar, and save him. Bataar clatters pots, tips items over, chills the air — never physically harming anyone — and Turgen writes the occurrences off as mundane, fearing abandonment. *Roleplaying:* friendly, outgoing, boisterous, serious only in danger; loves games and making friends, and has a codependent relationship with his hawk **Od**, whom he speaks to like an old spouse and who rarely obeys except in dire need.

> Note: the trader **Sayaka** was originally named **Bataar Badma** — the shared "Bataar" name is a thread a GM may draw between Turgen's curse and the wider Burning Sands.
